body:

[**geomlib**](https://github.com/brownnrl/euclid) is a TypeScript port
of Dr. David E. Joyce's *Geometry Applet* (Clark University, 1996,
Java version 2.2). It renders interactive Euclidean geometry diagrams
on an HTML5 `<canvas>`: drag a point, and every construction that
depends on it follows. The original Java applet illustrated Euclid's
*Elements*; this port does the same job in a modern browser, no JVM
required.

- [**Quickstart**](/geomlib/quickstart/) — build Euclid's Proposition I.1 from
  an empty canvas, one construction at a time, with the figure live at every
  step.
- [**Using geomlib diagrams**](/geomlib/using/) — how to work the figures:
  dragging points, the reset key, and hovering the letters in the text to
  light them up in the diagram.
- [**Constructions**](/geomlib/constructions/) — every construction the
  library implements, each with a figure you can drag and the element string
  that produced it.
- [**Slide transitions**](/geomlib/animations/) — the animations that step a
  figure from one slide to the next, each with a deck you can walk through.
- [**Example page**](/geomlib/example/) — a page built the way every page of
  the text is, whose own content describes each part of it.
- [**How this site is built**](/geomlib/colophon/) — where these pages were
  converted from, how a proposition is modelled so its proof can be stepped
  through, and the extensions written to carry Euclid's own numbering.
- [**Geometry Applet (archival)**](/geomlib/historical/) — Dr. Joyce's
  original 1996 documentation for the Java applet this library ports.

<figure class="diagram block">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="400" height="360" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="The icosahedron" src="propXIII16a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "The icosahedron",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;25,-1000;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;25,1000;0;0",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,0,40",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,0,320",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "ABmid;point;midpoint;A,B;0;0",
            "ABcirc;circle;radius;ABmid,B;0;0;0;0",
            "A1;point;perpendicular;A,B;0;0",
            "A2;point;extend;A,A1,A,A1;0;0",
            "A4;point;extend;A,A2,A,A2;0;0",
            "B1;point;perpendicular;B,A;0;0",
            "C;point;intersection;A,B,A4,B1;0;0",
            "D';point;perpendicular;C,A;0;0",
            "Dch;line;chord;C,D',ABcirc;0;0;0",
            "D;point;last;Dch;0;0",
            "a;point;free;220,180;0",
            "sphere;sphere;radius;a,ABmid,A;0;0;0;0",
            "X;point;sphereSlider;sphere,310,155,100",
            "Z;point;extend;X,a,X,a",
            "b;point;midpoint;B,D;0;0",
            "V;point;cutoff;a,X,b,B;0;0",
            "W;point;extend;V,a,V,a;0;0",
            "Vplane;plane;perpendicular;V,a;0;0;0;0",
            "Vcirc;circle;radius;V,D,B,Vplane;0;0;0;0",
            "L;point;circleSlider;Vcirc,240,30,-20",
            "LEcirc;circle;radius;L,W,Z,Vplane;0;0;0;0",
            "FE;line;bichord;Vcirc,LEcirc;0;0;0",
            "E;point;last;FE;0;0",
            "dec;polygon;regularPolygon;L,E,10,Vplane;0;0;0;0",
            "P;point;vertex;dec,3",
            "K;point;vertex;dec,4;0;0",
            "O;point;vertex;dec,5",
            "H;point;vertex;dec,6;0;0",
            "N;point;vertex;dec,7",
            "G;point;vertex;dec,8;0;0",
            "M;point;vertex;dec,9",
            "F;point;vertex;dec,10;0;0",
            "Q;point;perpendicular;E,Vplane,D,B",
            "R;point;perpendicular;F,Vplane,D,B",
            "S;point;perpendicular;G,Vplane,D,B",
            "T;point;perpendicular;H,Vplane,D,B",
            "U;point;perpendicular;K,Vplane,D,B",
            "LQR;polygon;triangle;L,Q,R",
            "MRS;polygon;triangle;M,R,S",
            "NST;polygon;triangle;N,S,T",
            "OUT;polygon;triangle;O,U,T",
            "QUP;polygon;triangle;Q,U,P",
            "LMR;polygon;triangle;L,M,R",
            "MNS;polygon;triangle;M,N,S",
            "NOT;polygon;triangle;N,O,T",
            "POU;polygon;triangle;P,O,U",
            "LPQ;polygon;triangle;L,P,Q",
            "QRZ;polygon;triangle;Q,R,Z",
            "RSZ;polygon;triangle;R,S,Z",
            "STZ;polygon;triangle;S,T,Z",
            "TUZ;polygon;triangle;T,U,Z",
            "UQZ;polygon;triangle;U,Q,Z",
            "LMX;polygon;triangle;L,M,X",
            "MNX;polygon;triangle;M,N,X",
            "NOX;polygon;triangle;N,O,X",
            "OPX;polygon;triangle;O,P,X",
            "PLX;polygon;triangle;P,L,X"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

The construction above is the icosahedron from @XIII.16 — twenty
equilateral triangles inscribed in a sphere of diameter *AB*. Drag
*A*, *B*, *a* (the sphere center), or *X* (a free point on the
sphere) to move the figure; the rest of the construction follows
automatically. See [Using geomlib diagrams](/geomlib/using/) for the
full set of interactions (free points vs sliders vs pivots, reset and
pop-out shortcuts).

### Use it on your own page

Drop the bundle in via CDN (no build step):

```html
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@brownnrl/geomlib@0.15.0/dist/bundle.js"></script>
```

Or install from npm:

```sh
npm install @brownnrl/geomlib
```

Then pair a `<canvas id="...">` with a `geomlib.init({...})` call
listing the elements you want constructed — see the Quickstart and
Constructions Reference linked above for the full vocabulary.

### Attribution

The original *Geometry Applet* was created by Dr. David E. Joyce
(Professor Emeritus, Department of Mathematics and Computer Science,
[Clark University](https://www.clarku.edu/)) between 1996 and 2020
for his online edition of Euclid's *Elements*. Nelson Brown received
Dr. Joyce's permission in May 2026 to republish the *Elements*
edition and to port the underlying applet to TypeScript as `geomlib`.
The library is open source under the
[MIT License](https://github.com/brownnrl/euclid/blob/main/LICENSE)
with joint copyright: © 1996–2020 David E. Joyce, 2019–2026 Nelson
Brown.

Joyce's original 1996 *Geometry Applet* documentation page is
preserved [here](/geomlib/historical/) for historical reference.

For how this edition itself is put together — where the pages were converted
from, how a proposition is modelled so its proof can be stepped through, and the
extensions written to carry Euclid's own numbering — see
[How this site is built](/geomlib/colophon/).

